#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055855} :activity/id #uuid "b9939b6d-b334-5155-b887-f965a9871210" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049002 :topic/id #uuid "d2783ca6-3cd7-54fc-baaa-eb47c3d52ff6" :topic/math-academy-id 58} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055595 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056074 :tutorial/content "Before we continue, we need a brief discussion on the concept of **integer divisibility.**\n\nSuppose $a$ and $b$ are integers. We write $a  \\mid b$ to mean that $\\boldsymbol a$ **divides** $\\boldsymbol b.$ Informally, this means the quotient of $\\dfrac b a$ is an *integer*, and there is *no remainder*. \n\nFor example, consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n5 \\,|\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$5$ divides $20$\". Moreover, this is a *true* statement because $20\\div 5 = 4$ is an integer. \n\nNow consider the following statement:\n\n$$\n6\\,  |\\, 20\n$$\n\nThis statement is *false* because $20\\div 6 = 3 {\\color{blue}{\\dfrac13}},$ which is *not* an integer. The correct statement is\n\n$$\n6 \\! \\not | \\: 20.\n$$\n\nThis statement reads, \"$6$ does not divide $20$\".\n\nFor now, this is all we need to understand the notation $a\\mid b.$ More formal discussions on integer divisibility will be given in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "ac10b43c-74e8-5752-9d06-2d746c31cb0b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9348 :tutorial/title "Divisibility Notation"} :step/id #uuid "e2dd3491-1e68-563f-850e-0f1a50254eee" :step/math-academy-id 161822 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056004 :tutorial/content "A **mathematical statement** is a sentence or mathematical expression that is either **true** or **false**.\n\nFor example:\n\n- The sentence, \"The sum of $1$ and $2$ is $3$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *true.*\n\n- Likewise, \"$2+2=5$\" is a mathematical statement because it is *false.*\n\nThe following examples are *not* mathematical statements:\n\n- \"Find the sum of $1$ and $2$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is an instruction.\n\n- \"What is the sum of $1$ and $2?$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false. It is a question.\n\n- Similarly, \"$6+9$\" is *not* a mathematical statement because it is neither true nor false.\n\nWe often use upper-case letters such as $P, Q,$ and $R$ to represent statements. For example:\n\n- $P:$ The sum of two even integers is odd.\n\n- $Q: 3 \\in \\mathbb Z$\n\n- $R: \\{2,4,6\\ldots\\}\\cup \\{1,3,5,\\ldots\\} = \\mathbb N$\n\nIn this case, the statements $Q$ and $R$ are true, whereas statement $P$ is false.\n\nWe often refer to the **truth value** of a mathematical statement. We use [math]T[/math] to denote \"true\" and [math]F[/math] to denote \"false.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "27451355-1bea-50f9-8b12-71af26b849e7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7961 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9dddcb7-93e7-5b0f-b0ab-3bede7e5408e" :step/math-academy-id 64628 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054817 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054364 :question/id #uuid "8ca6e60c-1411-5f6e-a9f4-d6209f847ddf" :question/math-academy-id "e-12676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f185774-4d55-515c-9032-8f6832c6999a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050915}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055504} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237955} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284701} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354473} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354474} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186404135}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Truth Value of a Statement"} :step/id #uuid "0fa5c65e-9772-56d9-b502-47729dbd3c60" :step/math-academy-id 64629 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054818 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054363 :question/id #uuid "db1f7851-43e9-5fee-9553-f9d6ead38b40" :question/math-academy-id "e-12675"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "228b0a63-bf0e-57bf-aa1e-ef5518236462" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055036} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055502} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354472} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399371}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Mathematical Statements"} :step/id #uuid "3e186ecb-bb2b-515b-9277-15fca8231263" :step/math-academy-id 64632 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055595}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054819 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054367 :question/id #uuid "d85fd26e-50f6-58f1-9e44-8df23863ee6a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12685"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eda1965-d655-5550-9bb5-13e7a9c1b406" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049887} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051033}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055034} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055035} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284708} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284709} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186378988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Statements Expressed Using Symbols"} :step/id #uuid "7738221b-b210-55a0-a14d-cb5a8da07c81" :step/math-academy-id 64636 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055859}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055859 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186056051 :tutorial/content "A **predicate** (or **open sentence**) of one variable is a sentence that contains a variable defined on some *universal set* and has the property that when we substitute a concrete value of the variable into the predicate, the result becomes a mathematical statement that is either *true* or *false*.\n\nFor example, consider the following predicate:\n\n> $x > 1$\n\nThis is a predicate because substituting concrete values for $x$ gives a mathematical statement.\n\nFor instance: \n\n- Substituting $x=2$ gives the *true* statement \"$2 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=5$ gives the *true* statement \"$5 > 1$\"\n\n- Substituting $x=0$ gives the *false* statement \"$0 > 1$\"\n\nWe denote predicates using capital letters and the corresponding variable in parentheses. In this example, we can write our predicate more formally as follows:\n\n> $P(x):$ $x > 1$\n\nAny predicate must be defined with its **universal set,** which is the set from which we are allowed to pull the concrete values of the variable. So, we could state our predicate more formally as\n\n> $P(x):$ $x > 1,$ $\\quad$ where $x \\in \\mathbb{R}.$\n\nThe universal set can often be identified from the context and doesn't need to be explicitly stated. For example, if we have the predicate \n\n> $n$ is divisible by 5,\n\nwe can assume that $n$ is defined on the set of all integers, i.e., $n \\in \\mathbb{Z}.$\n\nIn math, *any equation or inequality is a predicate*. However, a sentence containing a variable is not necessarily a predicate. For example, the sentence \n\n$\\qquad$ \"What is the sign of $y?$\"\n\nis *not* a predicate because substituting a concrete value for $y$ does not give a mathematical statement. Instead, it gives a question. \n\nSimilarly, \n\n> $z^2-5$\n\nis *not* a predicate either. It's just an expression." :tutorial/id #uuid "6162ecd9-50ee-52a9-9651-ba95a586ee9c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8303 :tutorial/title "Predicates"} :step/id #uuid "fe5e6113-0b7b-58a9-9078-465727b42948" :step/math-academy-id 64639 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055860}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055860 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054820 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054451 :question/id #uuid "a70765b4-08b9-5ebf-92f4-1b7c5266523e" :question/math-academy-id "e-13288"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "35eba041-5109-5855-b132-174c0271c168" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186055044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284733} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284735} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284736} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284737}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Predicates"} :step/id #uuid "da67dd80-78c6-52d8-867f-2aa94c264733" :step/math-academy-id 64640}] :activity/title "Statements and Predicates" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186054913}]}
